From 85a87923ab7b5286cf54f97329fbae2149288475 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: leo <leoschlosser@tutamail.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 06:48:14 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Input: mms114 - never report a pressed contact without
 pressure

The MMS152 in the Galaxy Note II reports a strength of 0 in single
reports of a contact that is still pressed, mostly while the finger
moves (113 such reports in 26 swipes). Android's input reader takes a
multi-touch contact whose pressure is 0 for a hovering, lifted finger,
so it ends the gesture there and starts a new one with the next
report: a swipe becomes a tap and a second touch. On the home screen
or the lock screen that adds up to a double tap, which puts the phone
to sleep, and swipes that start on a widget launch it.

Report at least 1 for a pressed contact.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/input/touchscreen/mms114.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/input/touchscreen/mms114.c b/drivers/input/touchscreen/mms114.c
index 33f54049a..8731a5a45 100644
--- a/drivers/input/touchscreen/mms114.c
+++ b/drivers/input/touchscreen/mms114.c
@@ -298,7 +298,13 @@ static void mms114_process_mt(struct mms114_data *data, struct mms114_touch *tou
 	if (pressed) {
 		touchscreen_report_pos(input_dev, &data->props, x, y, true);
 		input_report_abs(input_dev, ABS_MT_TOUCH_MAJOR, touch->width);
-		input_report_abs(input_dev, ABS_MT_PRESSURE, touch->strength);
+		/*
+		 * The strength of a pressed contact can read 0 (the MMS152 does
+		 * that in single reports while a finger moves). Android takes a
+		 * contact without pressure for a lifted finger and splits the
+		 * gesture into two.
+		 */
+		input_report_abs(input_dev, ABS_MT_PRESSURE, max_t(u8, touch->strength, 1));
 	}
 }
 
